# An .aiignore file follows the same syntax as a .gitignore file.
# .gitignore documentation: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitignore
# Junie will ask for explicit approval before view or edit the file or file within a directory listed in .aiignore.
# Only files contents is protected, Junie is still allowed to view file names even if they are listed in .aiignore.
# Be aware that the files you included in .aiignore can still be accessed by Junie in two cases:
# - If Brave Mode is turned on.
# - If a command has been added to the Allowlist — Junie will not ask for confirmation, even if it accesses - files and folders listed in .aiignore.

# 1) local environment and secrets
.git/
.env*
developer-local-settings/
.netrc
.npmrc
settings.xml

# 2) SSL/TLS certificates and private keys
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
*.truststore
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
secrets/

# 3) Build Artifacts and Dependencies ###
## While some Agents usually respects .gitignore, being explicit here ensures focus on source code instead of noisy logs and artefacts
node_modules/
target/
dist/
build/
.gradle/
.nx/
coverage/
test-results/
.quarkus/
.angular/
.m2/repository/
tmp/

# 4) System Files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# 5) Logs, reports (large/noisy; not needed for AI context)
logs/
*.log
surefire-reports/
spotbugs/

# 5a) Backup-Files
*.bak
*.bkp
*.backup
pom.xml.versionsBackup

# 6) Local override configs that may hold sensitive values
docker-compose.override.yml
docker-compose.override-*.yml

# 7) IDE and editor files
.idea/
*.iml

# 8) Helm/Charts values that may contain secrets (adjust if safe in your org)
charts/**/values*.yaml

# 9)  archives and packaged artifacts
*.zip
*.war
*.jar
*.tar
*.gz
*.bz2
*.7z

# NOTE: If you need the AI to read any of the protected files above for a
# specific task, temporarily comment out the line or grant explicit approval
# when prompted.
